A||||10||MooCow||What should I Do???||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||02-23-2000||09:40 AM||||Do you people think I should buy a Fiero for about $5,000.  Or should I buy one for around $2,000 to $3,000 to see if I even like Fiero's.  If I wait (which I do not want to do) I can get about $5,000 this summer I hope.  If not I could get one in the next few months.  I'm only 17 soon 18 so I really know nothing on how to fix the engine but would be more than willing to try to do the work myself so it is cheaper.  Let me know what you all think.<BR>                           Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||02-23-2000||11:08 AM||||Any Fiero is going to require some "tweaking" by virtue of the fact that even the newest ones are 12 years old. They are fun but you will have to fool with them on a semi-regular basis. $5k should buy a very nice one but it will still need upkeep. My $.02||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||02-23-2000||11:29 AM||||I'm wrestling with this very question myself right now.  Do I spend a bundle on a really nice Fiero, or spend less on one that needs some TLC?<P>Since this is your first Fiero (and I assume will be your only Fiero), I'd advise buying the nicest one you can afford.  If you're forced to choose between one that runs good and looks bad and one that looks good and runs bad, take the good-running one.  As long as the frame is unbent and rust-free, you can fix anything that's appearance-related.<P>One more point - the standard tranny is faster and more fun, but sooner or later, you will have to put a clutch in it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||02-23-2000||12:36 PM||||Chad,<P>Excellent points!  I would like to add that replacing a clutch is MUCH cheaper than replacing a TH-125.  And if abused by a prior owner, chances are that after a very long time you will have to replace one of those too.<P>MooCow,<P>5G's is a lot of cash.  If you are to spend that kind of money, the best advice I can give you is to buy from a Fiero-fanatic.  There are several reasons for this.  <P>1)  Usually we (Fiero-fanatics/Fieroholics) have a much better grasp on the true value of the car.  This should save you some $ on the initial purchase.  Of course this can backfire for those that have the rather common but expensive optional sentimental value.  Some of us think that our cars are worth what we think they are worth regardless of going price.  <P>2)  We have a much better idea of Fiero-specific problems or features that you should look out for.  <P>3)  Almost always our cars are in better shape than those that belong to persons that do not love their cars.  My fiancee always complains that I love my cars more than her... I must admit, I spend much more $ on them than her.<P>4)  We usually know what problems one can expect soon from our preventive maintenance schedule.  Certain parts only last so long. After years of ownership of a certain type of vehicle one learns to develop a good sense of how much life parts might or might not have.  <P>5)  You tend to find that the best cars have been maintained by their owners.  Most Fiero owners do their own work because the cars are not common and shops are often perplexed by "something different".  When most owners do the work you get a better job than when most shops do the work.  <P>The last bit of advice that I have for you is to stay away from MI cars if possible.  I have seen what MI winters & roads can do to them.  It is not pretty.  Also things that don't rust up there tend to bring a premium.  For example, I purchased a bare bones truck that had been used for "huntin'" down here in S.W. Ohio.  It was EXTREMELY rough and was what I considered a beater.  When I drove it up to MI, I received a couple of offers to buy it much higher than what I paid (and I thought I paid too much.. about 2X KBB value).  There was virtually no rust on a 15 yr. old truck and that raised the price significantly. <P>If I were looking into a car in that range, I would look up Ed Parks from the Fiero Factory in Toney, AL first.  He will custom build one for you if you so desire.  Furthermore being from AL, you know that it will not have seen ice, snow or more importantly salt in its lifetime.  I can see you know cruising down the road with a 88 GT with a pushrod 3.4L breathing heavily under the hood.  That would be one SWEET ride! <P>If you want to stay closer to home, I would reccomend Fiero Dave in Mosinee, WI; Paul Vargyas in Chicago, IL and Super X Motorsports (Greg) over in PA.  If these guys can't hook you up with a perfect Fiero no one can.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P><BR> <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000004||Tom88gt||02-23-2000||01:15 PM||||Moo Cow,<P>Since this will be your first Fiero, consider looking for a $500 beater (lots of them out there) and see if you really want to commit to the Fiero lifestyle.  While you are driving it, and trying to keep it alive, start looking for a good clean Fiero.  By the time you locate a good one (hard to find) you will really know it, and if its not as good as it looks you will also know because you learned so much from your temporary car.<P>Thats how I got my 914 Porsche.  I paid way too much for the first, because it was red and shiny.  My second one cost me half (was rust free-a virtual impossibility for 914s and still had the fuel injection-another rareity).  Why?  Because I learned so much from the first one.||||reg||12
Z||000005||mshill||02-23-2000||01:32 PM||||Moocow,  It really depends on what your intentions are for the car.  If you want to drive it and not work on it...well, good luck finding a 12-16 year old car in that good of repair.  If you want to drive and do a little work, buy as nice a car as possible.  If you want to tinker then don't pay any more than $1000 and do all the work yourself.<P>I got a very nice 86SE (clean interior and exterior) for $750 and have put about $2700<BR>into it to replace every bushing with poly, every coolant hose, radiator, heater core, rebuilt engine (including distributor), clutch, brakes, sensors, struts and shocks. For less than $3500 my car will very nearly be brand new.<P>In the process of rebuilding my engine (had 190,000 miles on it) I found it probably could have made it another 100,000 miles.  Little or no bearing or journal wear, great compression, etc.<P>If you are willing to do it yourself (big money saver) you can do anything to your car.  When I was 17/18 I rebuilt a Triumph GT-6 and had a very good experience.  Read a lot of books, ask a lot of questions and take your time doing the work and it will work.  Remember,  there are tips and tricks, but in general it is not rocket science.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||02-23-2000||02:20 PM||||The first time i drove a fiero was at a used car lot, those that say se hablo espanol.  It was a 84 or a 85 four cylinder but a stickshift.  The car wouldn't start, he had to jump it.  The body was fine except paint fade which you find on fieros that were not taken care of.  It needed some work done to it and the guy wanted about 1000.  I did take it for the test drive.  I always wanted a fiero since they came out in 84, seeing it parked at stores and drooling.  When you first sit in it you just think to yourself this is car i want, and not a rice car.  You sit really close to the ground.  When i took it out, the guy came with me, and i havn't really drivin stick except for motorcycles.  I took it on the side streets and it seems like your driving a go kart.  If i was you with 5000 i would get a stick.  The car is really fast and the suspension on these cars are great. <BR>If you want to test drive one go to one of those used car lots and see if they got a fiero.  The fiero is like a mix of the vette and the trans am.  I know they share the same alternator oh my.  You can buy a cheap fiero for 500, i got mine for 400 bucks.  14 years later after the fiero came out in 84, my mom sees a fiero down a few blocks from our house for sale, i rode my bike past to check it out.  I called up and the price was great.<BR>Its a 4 cylinder and an automatic but i still love the car.  The only major work i had was an oil pan leak, some sensor changes, and the regular tune up.  The paint is faded except for the sides.  In illinois, i usually see fieros up to 3000.  Those are the GT fieros with the six cylinders and stick shifts.  If you buy one around 3000, you will have money left over to buy some tools or get new tires, do a complete tune up.  No matter how much you spend, you will have to put work into the car.  After ten years, most sensors start going bad and need replacement.  If i had your money i would get the six, and a stick.  But first check out <A HREF="http://www.traderonline.com" TARGET=_blank>www.traderonline.com</A>  for a fiero search in your area to get an idea what they are going for.  You dont want to spend more money then what they are worth.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||02-23-2000||05:15 PM||||Make absolutely sure you get the important options.  I am referring to such things as:  steering wheel, doors, tires, transmission, motor, etc. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||02-23-2000||07:20 PM||||Monkey,<P>SMART A$$!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000009||HotFieroGT||02-23-2000||10:55 PM||||Hey MSHILL,<P>I thought I was the only Fiero guy who started out with Triumph GT-6's, Had a red 1973 and a red 1970 GT-6+.  Those were fun cars, but I have matured into Fieros!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ed||02-23-2000||11:54 PM||||A couple of Sprites taught me something about car repair. <P>I'm torn in my free advice about buying a cheap Fiero to get started, because I can see the value of getting a good one right off the bat. Another alternative is to find someone on this board who lives by you and is knowledgeable about Fieros to help you find one. Really, that is good advice for buying any used car: Information is power.  You could go out and buy any car and find out it was salvaged and bent and a waste of money.<P>There it is: Get a knowledgeable helper and buy the best one you can. ||||reg||1
